---
title: "Alert"
description: "Highlights an important message inline, in one of four variants."
tags: [feedback, css]
usage: |
  Add the `alert` class to a container. Set `data-variant` to `info` (default),
  `success`, `warning` or `danger` (`error` is an alias) to change its color.
accessibility: |
  - Use `role="status"` for messages that can wait to be announced and
    `role="alert"` only for urgent errors, which interrupt the screen reader.
  - A role on markup that is present on page load is not announced. Insert the
    alert, or its text, when the event happens.
  - Do not rely on color alone; start the message with a word such as
    "Warning" or "Error".
customProperties:
  "--alert-color": "Accent color; the variants set it."
  "--alert-padding": "Inner spacing."
  "--alert-border-width": "Width of the accent border on the start edge."
  "--alert-radius": "Corner radius."
  "--alert-background": "Background, mixed from the accent color."
  "--alert-margin-block": "Space above and below."
---

<link rel="stylesheet" href="./styles.css" media="screen">

<div class="alert" role="status" data-variant="info">
  <strong>Heads up!</strong> This is an informational alert.
</div>
<div class="alert" role="status" data-variant="warning">
  <strong>Warning!</strong> This is an warning alert.
</div>
<div class="alert" role="status" data-variant="success">
  <strong>Success!</strong> This is an success alert.
</div>
<div class="alert" role="alert" data-variant="danger">
  <strong>Error!</strong> Something went wrong.
</div>
